("note","NOTE","'Little' and 'a little' go with **uncountable** nouns; 'few' and 'a few' go with **countable** nouns. 'Only a little' / 'only a few' can add a negative feel: *Only a few students passed.*"),
("h3","Each, every, either, neither"),
("ul",["**Each** = one by one, for two or more: *Each of the boys got a prize.* **Every** = all, taken as a group, for three or more: *Every boy got a prize.*","*Each / every* + singular noun + singular verb: *Each child **has** a pen.* 'Every' cannot be used without a noun; use 'every one' or 'each'.","**Either** = one or the other of TWO; **neither** = not one nor the other of TWO. For more than two, use *any one / none*: *Any of the three; none of the three.*","*Either of the sisters **is** coming. Neither of the boys **was** absent.*","Either/neither need a singular verb in exams."]),
("h2","4.6 Numbers: Cardinal and Ordinal"),
("table",["Cardinal (how many)","Ordinal (which position)"],[
["one, two, three, four, five","first, second, third, fourth, fifth"],["eight, nine, twelve","eighth, ninth, twelfth"],["twenty, thirty","twentieth, thirtieth"],["twenty-one","twenty-first"],["hundred, thousand","hundredth, thousandth"]]),
("ul",["Ordinals take 'the': *the third floor, the twelfth chapter*.","When a number is used as an adjective of measure, the noun stays singular: *a five-rupee note, a two-year-old child, a ten-minute walk* (hyphenated, singular).","After a number, *dozen, hundred, thousand, million, score, pair* stay singular: *three hundred, two dozen*. Without a number they take 'of' and -s: *hundreds of people*.","Years: *in 1947* (nineteen forty-seven); decades: *the 1980s*.","*Next / last*: 'next week' (no article), 'the next day' (relative to a past time); 'last year' vs 'the last year of the century'."]),
("h2","4.7 Order of Determiners and Pre-determiners"),
("p","Pre-determiner (all, both, half) + determiner (the/my/this) + number + adjective + noun. *All the three boys, both my sisters, half the class, double the price, twice the size.* *Such* and *what*: *such a good idea, what a surprise.* Note *quite a, rather a, many a, such a*: article follows the word."),
("note","IMPORTANT","Common mistakes: (1) *Many a boys* → **Many a boy**. (2) *Few water* → **little water**. (3) *I have a few knowledge* → **a little knowledge**. (4) *Each of the boys have* → **has**. (5) *Less books* → **fewer books**. (6) *Everyday I walk* → **Every day** I walk. (7) *The both boys* → **Both the boys / The two boys.** (8) *Another two books* is acceptable; *the others book* → **the other book**."),
